    "body": "EICMA 2026 • MTPeak Stole Some of the Spotlight at EICMA 2026 With Insane Power and Retro Looks on Three Wheels\nHERE-SBD EV Index 2026 Reveals That a Quarter of EV Owners Consider a Hybrid for Their Next Car\n65 photos\nPhoto: Toyota\nPer a study made possible by Here Technologies and SBD Automotive, more battery-electric vehicle owners want a hybrid or a more heavily electrified internal combustion model as their next car. The share favoring hybrids, plug-in hybrids, and extended-range electric vehicles increased from 23 percent in 2025 to 28 percent this year, and that increase speaks volumes about consumer preferences.\nWhile it may not mean that electric vehicle owners have suddenly fallen out of love with their Teslas, the HERE-SBD EV Index 2026 found that more than a third are really considering hybridized vehicles. At the same time, 64 percent of surveyed BEV owners remain likely to purchase another fully electric model.\nThose numbers can easily coexist. Think about it this way: someone might appreciate the smooth acceleration, lower running costs, and quiet operation of a BEV while questioning whether the battery-only powertrain suits every journey they make. In other words, even BEV owners understand that battery-electric vehicles have their downsides.\nHere and SBD surveyed more than 4,000 drivers across the United States, the United Kingdom, Germany, France, Italy, Spain, India, and Australia, with the researchers examining a bunch of data. Such data includes ownership experiences, purchasing intentions, the charging infrastructure, and the factors that influence the BEV owner's next vehicle decision.\nSBD Automotive's Robert Fisher argues that manufacturers need to understand why existing BEV customers still consider alternatives. Put it this way: a plug-in hybrid is the better pick for longer trips because of its combustion engine. Plugging into a charging station and planning the trip with charging stops in mind is not necessary in this case, and the same can be said about EREVs.\nWhile the EREV may seem pretty similar to the PHEV, bear in mind that extended-range electric vehicles never use their combustion engines to drive the wheels directly. The combustion engine in an EREV is there only to generate electricity, which is fed to either the drive units or the high-voltage battery.\nPhoto: Toyota The EREV and PHEV, therefore, offer a different compromise between electric driving and the convenience of dinosaur juice. Looking at the bigger picture, the household situation also matters. The HERE-SBD EV Index 2026 found that 65 percent of surveyed BEV owners replaced their only vehicle with an electric model, effectively becoming electric-only households.\nSuch households rely on public charging networks on longer trips, and a broken charger is more than just a minor inconvenience if you are planning to drive 600 miles in a single day. A broken charger forces the driver to change plans, finding another charging location with fewer than 100 miles of range displayed by their car. I, for one, really hate those situations with a passion.\nSomething else I can't stand is how every single charging network where I live wants you to install their dedicated apps, forcing me to input my card details time and time again. And boy, I sure feel impatient when there's a line of battery-electric vehicles in front of me at the charging location of my liking. In many respects, I'm still identifying with team internal combustion, be it electrified or not.\nInterestingly enough, the HERE-SBD EV Index 2026 indicates that 68 percent of BEV owners are pleased with the charging situation. 67 percent, meanwhile, are pleased with their vehicle's driving range. From the standpoint of hybrid consideration, Italy leads the way, with 39 percent of BEV owners considering a hybrid as their next vehicle.\nThe US, UK, and Germany have much stronger BEV loyalty. The motivations behind BEV purchases have also changed, with better value for money ranking first among respondents. Lower running costs followed suit at 34 percent, while environmental benefits came third at 33 percent.\nAmong owners of internal combustion vehicles, only 17 percent identified electric models for their next purchase or lease.\nVia // SBD Automotive\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHybrid EV PHEV EREV study\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT He Bought a Tesla Model X Plaid in March, Already Got Rid of It and Will Buy a Ford F-150 Lightning",
